{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(First-step_analysis)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (First-step analysis)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Geometric_Series_and_Engineer&#39;s_Induction)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Geometric Series and Engineer's Induction)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Easiest_with_States)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Easiest with States)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Infinite_Series,_Sol_1_more_in_depth)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Infinite Series, Sol 1 more in depth)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Awnik repeatedly plays a game that has a probability of winning of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. The outcomes of the games are independent. What is the expected value of the number of games he will play until he has both won and lost at least once?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/d/1dd8d2e60b7861c7b3f387afe513a5d867e3edd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\frac{5}{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 3 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } \\frac{16}{5} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } \\frac{7}{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\frac{15}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"389\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the probability of a win be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and the probability of a loss be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. If the first game is a win, then we must find the expected number of further games to get a loss, which will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/e/20e8bbe4bee7c83c7ff4be58660ff2d03d6a9c42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"40\" />. In addition to the first game played, there will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/3/9837d78566f550d148dc62a921d2ee719884b72b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\frac{1}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"46\" height=\"42\" /> games played. Therefore, the expected number of games needed to get a win on game <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and then a loss is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/377533e2918132a5a6a016abef5840b904a18a45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p)\\left(1+\\frac{1}{q}\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"107\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Similarly, if the first game is a loss, we need to the find the expected number of further games to get a win, which will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/a/8da5e44b818c04ac581b6d32fd1070045f70f8df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"15\" height=\"42\" />. There will be a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/0/6d0d98fcc5bc5ddf78c463d75c085694cd5b24ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\frac{1}{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"46\" height=\"42\" /> games played. Therefore, the expected number of games needed to get a loss on game <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and then a win is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f83b7a51ac0b617fbdb1f4002833cc3f589062e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(q)\\left(1+\\frac{1}{p}\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"106\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>The answer is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/7/757fd176f619a952eef9c695e4a3895319a66875.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(p)\\left(1+\\frac{1}{q}\\right) + (q)\\left(1+\\frac{1}{p}\\right)\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/b/f7bf587a56e0b499c3624969e0036b68dd2ba0f1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(1+\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(1+3) = \\frac{5}{6} + \\frac{8}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}.\\]\" width=\"353\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~lprado\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28First-step_analysis.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(First-step_analysis)\">Solution 2 (First-step analysis)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (First-step analysis)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that if we stop after getting at least one win and one loss, then the stopping condition is the first state change. If we start with a win, then we count the expected number of rounds until we lose and vice versa.\n</p><p>We use first-step analysis:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/1/dc1a49418b4bff7e645c40c8bfb08689deb1a2fe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathbb{E}[T]=1+\\sum_{s\\in \\Omega}\\mathbb{P}(s)\\mathbb{E}[T-1|s]\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"38\" />\nHere, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/4/a847bcbc9d3a73f47dd8ac88a039454f8f9f067f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{P}(W)=\\frac{1}{3}, \\mathbb{P}(L)=\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"167\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Using first-step analysis on each condition expectation, by the Markov property\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/e/65e042d33313133ba5ff6f6a4bb17e88b0fb748d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathbb{E}[T|W]=1+\\mathbb{P}(W)\\mathbb{E}[T|W]+\\mathbb{P}(L)(0)=1+\\mathbb{P}(W)\\mathbb{E}[T|W]\\]\" width=\"461\" height=\"18\" />\nSolving, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/0/530756f7cd945c989cd7c4f14bf2d0049adf520d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[T|W]=\\frac{1}{1-\\mathbb{P}(W)}=\\frac{1}{\\mathbb{P}(L)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"232\" height=\"43\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/2/f22221c29077d1d9d34a4ae343f73976beaa5299.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[T|L]=\\frac{1}{\\mathbb{P}(W)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"127\" height=\"43\" />. Write <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/e/47ec9c63d85ae3a1e36519e9aaba8f31ac6b2bcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=\\mathbb{P}(W)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/6/c36dc2b41db6717b12f3d0b69dd3b9bb90e50a45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q=\\mathbb{P}(L)=1-p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"137\" height=\"20\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/a/43aba3e43397c79ad513771d13a9d197621b01ff.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathbb{E}[T]=1+\\mathbb{P}(W)\\mathbb{E}[T|W]+\\mathbb{P}(L)\\mathbb{E}[T|L]=1+p\\left( \\frac{1}{q} \\right)+q\\left( \\frac{1}{p} \\right)=1+\\frac{p}{q}+\\frac{q}{p}=1+\\frac{p^2+q^2}{pq}\\]\" width=\"648\" height=\"43\" />\nWe can simplify using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/544713fef858f2ef34621b8212a1d3e27ef23a0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p+q)^2=1=p^2+q^2+2pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"225\" height=\"21\" />, giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/3/e835335bc99310d3868ac26ac77504a0437c94d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2+q^2=1-2pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" />. Therefore,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/1/381b9929a67c1aa4799f6ab22d2a5855c80ba790.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathbb{E}[T]=1+\\frac{1-2pq}{pq}=1+\\frac{1}{pq}-2=\\frac{1}{pq}-1\\]\" width=\"338\" height=\"40\" />\nSubstituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c92f0a602610162810404747ac5df59e2422a51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"46\" height=\"37\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/a/66ab8883ba806dcf637e5d381977de8dfa040cd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q=\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"38\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/d/aaddeb5b0dc84d15219574a1b2d83e32b1203abb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } \\frac{7}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"153\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~imosilver\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Geometric_Series_and_Engineer.27s_Induction.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Geometric_Series_and_Engineer's_Induction)\">Solution 3 (Geometric Series and Engineer's Induction)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Geometric Series and Engineer&#39;s Induction)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We first conduct a little casework regarding the total number of games played.\n</p><p>2 games played: 2 * (1/3 * 2/3 + 2/3 * 1/3) = 8/9\n</p><p>3 games played: 3 * ((2/3)^2 * 1/3 + (1/3)^2 * 2/3) = 18/27\n</p><p>4 games played: 4 * ((2/3)^3 * 1/3 + (1/3)^3 * 2/3) = 40/81\n</p><p>5 games played: 5 * ((2/3)^4 * 1/3 + (1/3)^4 * 2/3) = 90/243\n</p><p>etc...\n</p><p>We observe that the pairs (8/9,40/81) and (18/27,90/243) all have a common ratio of 5/9. We speculate that the sum can be comprised of two infinite geometric series, all with a common ratio of 5/9, one with a first term of 8/9 and one with a first term of 18/27. Computing the sum of the two yields 2 and 3/2, respectively. Thus, the answer is 2 + 3/2 = 7/2.\n</p><p>Note: I just realized that this method is flawed, the later cases don't match this geometric pattern, yet it still achieves the right answer\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Easiest_with_States.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Easiest_with_States)\">Solution 4 (Easiest with States)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Easiest with States)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/8/248a4cb7586a8b784daed1a896a6f07cac09671f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_0, E_{1W}, E_{1L}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"18\" /> denote the expected number of remaining games needed from the states of no wins or losses, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0aeac561619dcee65a2b45d737a79892aa0ebf8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" /> win + no losses, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0aeac561619dcee65a2b45d737a79892aa0ebf8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" /> loss + no wins. Then we may write\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/e/f3e4689c4b56ae139b03f21687c48dbd2cf0e996.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E_0 = 1 + \\frac{1}{3} E_{1W} + \\frac{2}{3} E_{1L}\\]\" width=\"188\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/b/54ba9fe47a723d257809c014b6e2ec612ab3260f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E_{1W} = 1 + \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 0 + \\frac{1}{3} E_{1W} \\rightarrow E_{1W} = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\" width=\"295\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/c/afcd216f7f8574ea75a0d8721f0200a47bcaee5b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E_{1L} = 1 + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 0 + \\frac{2}{3} E_{1L} \\rightarrow E_{1L} = 3\\]\" width=\"276\" height=\"37\" />\nSolving gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/b/4db7df17d216e2e23f5bdadbac55e3d4850e98c5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E_0 = 1 + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 = \\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\\]\" width=\"221\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~Alex2\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the game is played for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> turns, we can easily observe the following two cases.\n</p><p>Either he wins the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/e/d0e80c6b6a84ad4015ea436a915435d69edbdd96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> turns and loses on the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th turn\nOr he loses the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/e/d0e80c6b6a84ad4015ea436a915435d69edbdd96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> turns and wins on the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th turn\n</p><p>This is motivated from looking at smaller cases and noting that if the goal has to be achieved in exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> moves, he can't immediately start with winning the first turn and losing the second turn since that finishes the game in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> turns and not necessarily <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> turns.\n</p><p>The expected value is just the probability multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. \n</p><p>So we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/b/c5b9916e82718a677bba80d6eea930dddbc0c101.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n((\\frac{1}{3})^{n - 1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} + (\\frac{2}{3})^{n - 1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) = \\frac{n(2 + 2^{n - 1})}{3^n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"328\" height=\"41\" />. \n</p><p>This expression needs to be summed from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> to infinity. \n</p><p>We rewrite the expression as \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/d/93d5e452439d12f98805e93bfd0ebef64392afa8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2 \\cdot n(\\frac{1}{3})^n + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot n(\\frac{2}{3})^{n}\\]\" width=\"163\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We claim that the standard arithmetico-geometric series <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/0/ad0e9b26e71a2c80f52544450ef9e8fcfb27b5b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nx^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> has a sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/0/8e09517559a42cb2511958a6a3a3600e85c435be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{(1 - x)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"69\" height=\"39\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/6/796af557812a3e6b3f7bff6d89875f9d7721d320.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x| &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p>Proof: We start with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/0/520f72123cb95ddc1f4959ef8990b82d0baa010a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + x + x^2 + \\cdots = \\frac{1}{1 - x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"197\" height=\"40\" />. Taking the derivative with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> on both sides gives\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/a/b2ad32d6b9f7158e75268465b338b16c14482016.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2x + 3x^2 + \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"148\" height=\"18\" /> which is just the sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6a40384ed0abb94f530997c806a84d5760d87e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nx^{n - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"17\" /> and this equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a3e45dd09fd3a373f2f38b62296a722105757e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{(1 - x)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"69\" height=\"43\" />. To match <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/0/ad0e9b26e71a2c80f52544450ef9e8fcfb27b5b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nx^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> we multiply both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and get that the sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/0/ad0e9b26e71a2c80f52544450ef9e8fcfb27b5b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nx^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/6/c766f9fce6070981e62f805a7cf7294baed4498e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> to infinity is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/0/8e09517559a42cb2511958a6a3a3600e85c435be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{(1 - x)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"69\" height=\"39\" />. However, we're summing from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/6/c766f9fce6070981e62f805a7cf7294baed4498e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> so we subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/274332d0d28515eceee7ac5068d2578181e3381d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\cdot x^1 = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"77\" height=\"17\" /> from both sides to get the desired sum of\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/8/3e89ab801cdf857cbaba3543158fdaf827e70b3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x^2(2 - x)}{(1 - x)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"80\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Now we plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/f/46fbdac9561476d9fa69c150b086303843a5832c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"50\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> to get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a69002e769ce250ebfa07319fcf6dcf9b3fe807.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})^2 \\cdot (2 - \\frac{1}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})^2} = \\frac{5}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"167\" height=\"49\" /> for the first part of the sum.\n</p><p>Now for the second part we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4c1f1ced98d2b658abf587398bcf3837e850da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}}{(1 - \\frac{2}{3})^2} - \\frac{2}{3}) = \\frac{8}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"182\" height=\"49\" /> for this second part.\n</p><p>Finally we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/3/c43432e15050ff102ee1771d974a57a7d967fdaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{6} + \\frac{8}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"99\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~ilikemath247365\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Infinite_Series.2C_Sol_1_more_in_depth.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Infinite_Series,_Sol_1_more_in_depth)\">Solution 6 (Infinite Series, Sol 1 more in depth)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Infinite Series, Sol 1 more in depth)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> the probability of a win and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> the probability of a loss. \n</p><p>Assume he begins with a win. That is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> chance. After the first game, he can lose with expectation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/c/dec195a05d3203ca82d198fc9c963bcb81b3c2dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" />, win then lose with expectation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/d/abd2b31e6ccda58585b85f5b6c50bf0286973c85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"17\" />, win then win then lose with expectation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/c/d0cbe3692076be2e9b9dbff4383fe66674cf9556.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3p^2q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" />, so on so forth.\nThat is a \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/2/ee2a58793c2c837f6223efee1c42c60f041d1243.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} np^{k-1}q = q \\cdot \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} np^{n-1} = q \\cdot \\frac{1}{q^2} = \\frac{1}{q}\\]\" width=\"305\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>expectation. In addition to the win, the expected number of games is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/b/f0b5e53cf7d5c9c7a1bb90f567b2d04581f091af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p(1 + \\frac{1}{q})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"70\" height=\"42\" />.\n</p><p>Assume a loss. That is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> chance. After the first game, he can win with expectation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/a/7ba1652564c64f2b4c04e226428fa326493c0c69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" />, lose-win <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/3/3a3e695dbb98da12d8ea2c29ae79ee94cad6d84f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2qp$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"17\" />, so on which gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/a/8da5e44b818c04ac581b6d32fd1070045f70f8df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"15\" height=\"42\" />. The expected result here is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/1/ed123a176ffc52f23429bb6d0adf784b89e83a8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q(1+\\frac{1}{p})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"69\" height=\"42\" />.\n</p><p>The requested result is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/b/f7bf587a56e0b499c3624969e0036b68dd2ba0f1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(1+\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(1+3) = \\frac{5}{6} + \\frac{8}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}.\\]\" width=\"353\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/9l4gI28Q9O8\">https://youtu.be/9l4gI28Q9O8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_MadMath\">Video Solution 2 by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/SHNLPRQIR5w\">https://youtu.be/SHNLPRQIR5w</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B\">2025 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 19\">Problem 19</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_1\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_12_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 12 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 12 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018030612\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [